ation While a Work Item-level analysis is pending, a comparison between GCF Conformance Assessment Table 3.101.0_20260508_r004 [3] and RAN5 specs reveals that the proportion of unadopted RAN5 RRM test cases by industry certification is significantly higher than expected. Table 1 provides an example for LTE RRM: the number of GCF-listed LTE RRM test cases compares with the total number of LTE RRM test cases listed in 3GPP TS 36.521-2 V19.2.0 (2026-06) [4]. Table 2 provides an example for 5G RRM: the number of GCF-listed 5G RRM test cases compares with the total number of 5G RRM test cases listed in 3GPP TS 38.522 V19.4.0 (2026-06) [5]. Table 1 LTE RRM Table 2 5G RRM Observation 1: A comparison between the GCF Conformance Assessment Table and RAN5 specs reveals that the proportion of RAN5 test cases not adopted by GCF is significantly higher than expected. This indicates a substantial misalignment between RAN5 standardization outputs and actual industry requirements, resulting in unnecessary workload and resource inefficiency within RAN5. As the introduction of GCF test cases is based on GCF WIs, which are derived from RAN5 WIs, this indicates the proportion of RAN5 Work Items lacking large-scale commercial deployment is considerably high. Conclusion 1: The proportion of RAN5 Work Items (WIs) lacking large-scale commercial deployment is considerably high. Efficiencies of PRD21 A comparison between the GCF Conformance Assessment Table 3.101.0_20260508_r004 [3] and RAN5 PRD21 5G NR bands and CADC configurations list_v1.17.0 [6] reveals that the proportion of unreferenced RAN5 completed configurations is significantly higher than anticipated. Table 3 provides an example for DL EN-DC (PC3): the number of GCF-listed DL EN-DC (PC3) versus the total number of DL EN-DC (PC3) marked as completed in 3GPP PRD21 v1.17.0 [6]. Table 3 DL EN-DC (PC3) PTCRB bands are not included in this statistic. Even with the extra PTCRB-specific DL EN-DCs (PC3)s added, the pool of configurations with real testing and certification demands is still far smaller than those marked as completed in RAN5 PRD21. Moreover, even among the configurations listed in GCF, a significant portion lack actual testing and certification demands. Observation 2: A comparison between the GCF Conformance Assessment Table and RAN5 PRD21 reveals that the proportion of unreferenced RAN5 completed configurations is significantly higher than anticipated. This indicates a substantial misalignment between RAN5 standardization outputs and actual industry requirements, resulting in unnecessary workload and resource inefficiency within RAN5. Conclusion 2: A substantial number of configurations remain undeployed even after operators have explicitly expressed interest. Proposals for 6G To ensure a reasonable set of 6G testing with commercial/deployment plan considered, we propose the following: Proposal 1: All stakeholders shall conduct prudent assessments during 6G WI initiation phase. Proposal 2: Test applicability specs shall provide clear guidance for test and certification activities. Redundancy must be minimized through clear categorization of each test: Category A: Band-dependent (test across all bands); Category B: Band-agnostic but FDD/TDD dependent (test one FDD band plus one TDD band); Category C: Band-agnostic and FDD/TDD-agnostic (test one single band). Proposal 3: CA Fallback rule shall be applied from day one of 6G, e.g., for RF, if requirements are same, only CA with the highest supported number of carriers needs to be tested, lower number of carriers CA and single carriers can be skipped. Proposal 4: The following general RRM testing principles are proposed for the upcoming 6G specs to reduce redundant testing: RRM test cases related to absolute power measurement, e.g.,

